  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts a coastal marine system before and during an oil-spill disaster. In the pre-disaster view, the shoreline transitions from marshes to beaches and then down the continental slope into nearshore and deep-sea benthic habitats. Water and sediments look clean, with marsh vegetation, beach invertebrates, fish in the water column, and coral-like deep benthic communities indicating a relatively intact ecosystem.\n\nIn the disaster view, oil spreads across multiple connected habitats at once. Dark surface slicks cover offshore waters and reach the beach and marsh edge, where oil appears to coat vegetation and shoreline organisms. Below the surface, oil also seems to move into sediments and along the seabed, forming patches labeled as oil in sediment and subsurface oil. This contamination extends from the shallow nearshore benthos down into the deep-sea benthos, where the plume appears linked to a leaking seafloor wellhead. Fish remain present in the water column, but the main message is that pollution is no longer confined to the surface: it affects marshes, beaches, shallow benthic areas, and deep-sea habitats simultaneously. Overall, the figure emphasizes the broad spatial reach of an offshore spill from coast to deep ocean.",
